  BlockML Core — enum type branch (closed value sets)

  FQN: org.blockml.type.EnumType
  See docs/17_type_system_bml.md — EnumType
       docs/02_mbml.md — Enum types · Enum value references
       docs/04_types.md — Enum types · Enum value references
       docs/00_bom_core.md — EnumType property values
       docs/16_framework_base_types.md — ValueType branch
-->
<block xmlns="http://blockml.org/schema/block" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/schema/block https://blockml.org/schemas/core/block.xsd"
  xmlns:btype="org.blockml.type"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.core">

  <idea>Branch for closed sets of named literal values.</idea>

  <documentation>
    Abstract ValueType branch for closed enum ValueTypes — finite sets of named literal values.
    Unqualified name EnumType resolves to org.blockml.type.EnumType.

    One of three language-level ValueType kinds (PrimitiveType · StructType · EnumType). EnumType
    is a specialized StructType — it adds a finite list of allowed values via the default values
    aggregation. Concrete EnumTypes declare their allowed values as Block instances in values — no
    special enum syntax. Examples: Direction, Face, StudType, BaseCutoutType.

    Each enum value is a Block instance of the EnumType with an id (bml:id) and a value property
    holding the runtime/serialization literal. The editor renders EnumType properties as a
    SelectBox; the compiler validates values against values.

    Properties typed with an EnumType must not carry &lt;suggestions&gt; — valid values come
    exclusively from values → BML_SUGGESTIONS_ON_ENUM. Properties may still carry
    &lt;constraints&gt; where a compatible Constraint sub-type exists.

    Enum value references in property values (defaultValue, composition attributes, expressions):
    when the property type is an EnumType, qualified references may omit the {EnumTypeFQN}::
    prefix — STANDARD is equivalent to com.machineblocks.type.BaseCutoutType::STANDARD when the
    expected type is known from the property declaration. The short form is invalid for untyped
    strings. The validator resolves tokens against values by id; unknown values → validation
    error.

    Id reference (STANDARD / FQN::STANDARD) and value literal (standard) are distinct surface
    forms; both must match a declared values entry.

    Domain XSD enums remain accepted during migration with BML_LEGACY_TYPE_SOURCE warning — new
    enums should be defined as BML EnumType blocks.

    Used wherever BlockML declares or validates closed value sets:
    • Property &lt;type&gt; references (for example type="com.machineblocks.type.BaseCutoutType")
    • defaultValue and instance attribute values on EnumType properties
    • Expression enum literals where the expected EnumType is known from context
    • Library _blocks export alongside other ValueType definitions

    See 17_type_system_bml.md — EnumType · 02_mbml.md — Enum types · 04_types.md ·
    00_bom_core.md — EnumType property values.
  </documentation>
  <name>EnumType</name>
  <type>btype:EnumType</type>
  <baseType>btype:StructType</baseType>
  <abstract />

  <aggregations>
    <values
      type="bml:Self[]"
      default="true">
      <documentation>
        Default composition aggregation for enum literal Block instances. Each child is a Block
        instance of this EnumType with bml:id (reference token) and value (serialization
        literal). Valid values for properties typed with this EnumType come exclusively from
        values — authored in &lt;composition aggregation="values"&gt; with no special enum
        syntax. See 17_type_system_bml.md — EnumType.
      </documentation>
    </values>
  </aggregations>
</block>
